<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toghon Temür</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fled <a href="/years/1368">1368</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Toghon Temur · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toghon Temür was the last emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. His reign was marked by factional conflict, natural disasters and widespread rebellion, including the Red Turban uprisings. In 1368, as Ming forces approached the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, he fled north to the Mongolian steppe, marking the end of Yuan rule in China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toghon-temur.jpg" alt="Toghon Temür" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 松照庵 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%85%83%E9%A0%86%E5%B8%9D%E5%A6%A5%E6%AD%A1%E5%B8%96%E6%9C%A8%E5%85%92_Emperor_Shun_of_Yuan_Toghon_Tem%C3%BCr.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Toghon Temür was born on 25 May 1320 to Kusala, then in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. His mother, Mailaiti, was a descendant of Arslan Khan, a chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-two-capitals">War of the Two Capitals</a>, which followed the death of Yesün Temür in 1328, Kusala died under suspicious circumstances and his younger brother was restored to the throne as Emperor Wenzong. Toghon Temür was removed from court and exiled, first to Goryeo and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>. When Wenzong died in 1332, the succession took months to settle, and Toghon Temür was enthroned only in 1333.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The early reign was dominated by powerful ministers, first El Temür and then Bayan, who carried out political purges and suspended the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a>. In 1340 the emperor allied with Bayan's nephew Toqto'a and removed Bayan in a coup. Under Toqto'a the purges were reversed, the examinations restored and many Chinese literati recalled. The official histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, Jin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties were completed in 1345.</p><p>From the mid-1340s droughts, floods and famines struck the countryside, while the government faced currency depreciation and falling tax revenues. In 1348 illicit salt dealers rebelled against the state salt monopoly, setting off further revolts. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a> erupted in 1351, Toqto'a raised a large army against it, but Toghon Temür dismissed him in 1354. The emperor gradually withdrew from active governance, and in 1364 the warlord Bolad Temür occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a> and expelled the crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayushiridara">Ayushiridara</a>. The fighting further weakened central authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> and sent his armies north. In 1368 Toghon Temür abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a> for his summer capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a>, which the Ming took in 1369. He retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingchang">Yingchang</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, and died there on 23 May 1370. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayushiridara">Ayushiridara</a> succeeded him and withdrew the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>. The remnant court, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a>, continued to claim the title of Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>The Ming held that the Yuan had forfeited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a> and gave him the posthumous name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shun-emperor">Emperor Shun</a>, meaning compliant. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a>'s temple name for him was Huizong. He reigned for 35 years, the longest of any Yuan emperor after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>, and patronised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. Historians debate how far the dynasty fell from structural problems and how far from his withdrawal from government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toghon Temür” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last Yuan emperor, <b>Toghon Temür</b>, fled Dadu that year before Ming forces under Xu Da entered the city with little resistance, and retreated to the Mongolian steppe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The last emperor, <b>Toghon Temür</b>, fled north to the steppe in 1368 as the Ming army approached.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1368 Ming Empire armies approached Dadu, and <b>Toghon Temür</b>, last emperor of the Yuan Dynasty, fled to Shangdu and then to Yingchang.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingchang">Yingchang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.25,116.46&amp;year=1368" aria-label="Yingchang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7357 629 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Toghon Temür"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7807 899h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7807 899h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.25,116.46&amp;year=1368">What was here in 1368? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
